-I developed the Walker Two-Layer (W2L) controller from the Li--Deng walker. Its central pattern generator (CPG) produces rhythmic motor commands. In W2L each leg has its own RG, and connections between the two set their relative timing. Flexor and extensor PF populations distribute that timing to the hip and knee motor neurons. The knee PF populations also contribute to ankle recruitment. Heel and toe contact signals return to the spinal circuit, so removing those connections provides a way to test how foot contact contributes to alternation (Appendix~\ref{sec:app_w2l_perturbations}).
