 \section{Building the Mechanical Model from Robot CAD}\label{sec:sns_cad_methods}
 
-The goal was to run a neural controller on the robot we intend to build. Gait2392 supplied a human reference and a skeleton for exploring muscle routes. Our SOLIDWORKS assembly supplied the segments, joints, and attachment locations of the physical design, including its four-bar knees. I brought that assembly into both MuJoCo and Simscape Multibody. In MuJoCo, STL surface meshes gave the rigid parts their shapes. The MJCF model specified their masses, inertias, joint constraints, and actuator attachments. In Simscape Multibody, the CAD import generated the bodies and joints as Simulink blocks (Chapters~\ref{ch:background} and~\ref{ch:torque}).
-
-I aligned the assembly to the pelvis datum so that $x$ points anteriorly, $y$ superiorly, and $z$ to the robot's right. I represented the knee closures as constraints in MuJoCo. As a knee flexes, each attachment moves with its rigid body, changing the direction of actuator force and its moment arm about the joint (Equation~\ref{eq:momentarm_def}). This is the geometry that a neural controller encounters when it recruits an actuator. Appendix~\ref{sec:app_cad_supported_stance} describes the contact experiment on this model.
-
-SNS-Simscape supplies neural and actuator blocks in Simulink. Simscape Multibody supplies the mechanical bodies and joints. With the MathWorks MuJoCo--Simulink blockset, MuJoCo advances the mechanical model while Simulink advances the controller \citep{mathworks_mujoco_blockset}. Section~\ref{sec:sns_bridge_methods} demonstrates this exchange by sending muscle commands into MuJoCo and reading back changes in joint angle and muscle length. The CAD stepping trial in Section~\ref{sec:sns_ground_results} used the separate MuJoCo implementation.
+The robot we intend to build has four-bar knees and attachment locations defined in its SOLIDWORKS assembly. I brought that assembly into both MuJoCo and Simscape Multibody. In MuJoCo, STL surface meshes describe the shape of each rigid part, and MJCF specifies its mass, inertia, constraints, and actuator attachments. In Simscape Multibody, the CAD import produces bodies and joints as Simulink blocks (Chapters~\ref{ch:background} and~\ref{ch:torque}).
+
+I aligned the assembly to the pelvis datum with $x$ anterior, $y$ superior, and $z$ to the robot's right, and represented the knee closures as constraints in MuJoCo. As the knee flexes, each attachment moves with its rigid body, changing the line of action and moment arm (Equation~\ref{eq:momentarm_def}). The controller must recruit the actuator through that changing geometry.
+
+The imported Simscape Multibody model can connect to neural and actuator blocks in Simulink. Alternatively, Simulink can run the controller while MuJoCo advances the mechanical model through the interface described in Section~\ref{sec:sns_bridge_methods}. The CAD stepping trial in Section~\ref{sec:sns_ground_results} used the separate MuJoCo implementation.
 
