-A knee that flexes too little gives no direct indication of where the error arose. To locate the timing error, I examined how syn6 distributes muscle recruitment among six pattern-formation channels in each leg of the Gait2392 human model. The six recruitment patterns came from a separate calculation that worked backward from reference joint motion and ground forces to the muscle activations needed during a stride (Appendix~\ref{sec:app_fsa_design}). In the original circuit, the fifth channel continued to excite its motor neurons during the part of the cycle when it should have been silent. Changing the connection strengths improved the fit to muscle recruitment while leaving that timing error in place. Recording the signal leaving the channel showed which part of the controller needed revision.
-
-I changed how the channels started and stopped their bursts. The revised circuit met its timing targets with prescribed sensory inputs calculated from the reference movement. When the moving, suspended legs supplied their own feedback, a subsequent repair kept the second channel to one burst per cycle, but the third still produced three or four bursts. The trial completed with the knee-extension limit respected, but its rhythm missed the acceptance criteria. The phase-averaged knee curves spanned \ang{19} on the left and \ang{34} on the right, compared with about \qtyrange{70}{72}{\degree} in the walking references (Figure~\ref{fig:sns_syn6_overlay}). Individual cycles varied, so the average curves conceal some of the knee motion. The extra bursts identify a remaining timing error in the controller. Neither foot supported weight in this trial. Appendix~\ref{sec:app_pf_timing} gives the timing criteria and trial conditions.
+The syn6 controller distributes muscle recruitment through six PF channels per leg. Each channel should excite groups of motor neurons during a particular part of the stride, as estimated from reference motion and ground forces (Appendix~\ref{sec:app_fsa_design}). The fitting procedure could improve the combined motor output even when an individual channel was mistimed. I developed a timing test that aligns each channel's outgoing synaptic signal with the rhythm and compares its active interval with the target. The fifth channel continued to excite its motor neurons throughout the cycle on both sides, including when it should have been silent. Refitting the motor connections left this error in place.
+
