 \section{Discussion}\label{sec:sns_discussion}
 
-\subsection{What Can Be Transferred Between Models}
-
-A muscle-force comparison can identify a mechanical discrepancy before neural parameters are adjusted. The knee and cup experiments then show what changes when sensory current reaches a motor pool in a model whose load and actuator model are known. When a foot touches the ground, the same comparison requires a record of contact, actuator force, and external assistance. The same distinction matters when moving a published controller to another body. The controller of \citet{dirusso_investigating_2023} reproduced stepping with its original OpenSim model in SCONE, open-source software for simulating and optimizing movement. Its neural equations could be reproduced under prescribed inputs in the Gait2392 implementation, but the new body fell when it supplied its own feedback. Appendix~\ref{sec:app_dirusso_attempt} shows why the timing of foot contact matters. The revised interface signaled heel strike once, when the foot first took load. Repeatedly signaling heel strike while the foot remained planted would keep restarting the controller's step cycle.
-
-The W2L sensory intervention illustrates the value of separate outcome measures. All three conditions produced forward movement, but heel-strike phase fell outside the accepted range only after the combined removal. In syn6, an acceptable pattern of motor recruitment likewise concealed a PF channel that remained active throughout the cycle. Recording the signal at the receiving layer distinguished a successful output fit from a circuit that performed its intended function.
-
-\subsection{Separating Neural Drive from Harness Assistance}
-
-The human drive-weaning experiment separated a particular descending input from the mechanical assistance. The human model continued to step in place under its virtual harness when supplementary left and right drive reached zero. The shared descending input remained active. In a separate W2L robot experiment the model progressed with reduced supplementary drive while the harness remained engaged (Appendix~\ref{sec:sns_controller_comparison}).
-
-Suspension removes ground loading. Rails and guides permit contact but carry part of the weight and constrain motion. The evidence for the BiW2Lvar+ air stages, the W2L pathway removal, W2L108 treadmill stepping, and the CAD model's contact handover therefore applies to four distinct experimental conditions. No experiment in this program established harness-free walking.
-
-The force model also determines what a controller test can establish. The simplified knee actuator isolates the response to a sensory pulse. The BPA20 arm model tests whether the feedback circuit can recruit enough pressure-dependent force as a load increases. Specifying ideal actuator forces on the CAD model isolates the demands of contact and swing clearance. Applying its controller to physical BPAs introduces a further question about attainable force at each measured length and pressure.
+\subsection{Transferring Neural Controllers Between Mechanical Models}
+
+Reproducing a neural circuit under prescribed inputs is a first test of transfer. When the circuit drives a new body, its movement must generate the inputs that trigger the next step. The controller of \citet{dirusso_investigating_2023} reproduced stepping with its original OpenSim model in SCONE, software for simulating and optimizing movement. The Gait2392 implementation reproduced the neural equations under prescribed inputs, yet the new body fell when it generated its own feedback. A record of foot contact exposed one important interface choice. Heel strike must be signaled when the foot first takes load. Repeating that signal while the foot remains planted restarts the step cycle (Appendix~\ref{sec:app_dirusso_attempt}).
+
+A transfer test therefore needs measurements on both sides of a connection. All three W2L conditions produced forward motion, yet only the combined sensory deletion disrupted heel-strike phase. In syn6, acceptable motor recruitment concealed a PF channel that stayed active throughout the cycle. Measuring contact in W2L and transmitted synaptic activity in syn6 revealed which behavior the combined output concealed.
+
+\subsection{Distinguishing Neural Drive from Mechanical Support}
+
+The Gait2392 model continued stepping in place when its supplementary left and right drive reached zero. Its shared descending input and pelvic harness remained active. In a separate W2L experiment, the robot model progressed with reduced supplementary drive while its harness still supported it (Appendix~\ref{sec:sns_controller_comparison}). Lowering neural drive and reducing mechanical assistance test different requirements for stepping.
+
+The support determines what coordination a trial can establish. With the feet held clear, the model can alternate its legs without supporting its weight. The W2L pathway trials tested contact timing under pelvic support. On the treadmill, passive rails carried part of the W2L108 model's weight. The CAD model transferred load between its feet while the harness supplied lift and steadied the pelvis. Unaided walking remains unestablished across these models.
+
+The muscle model determines how a change in recruitment reaches the joint. In the biological knee, an increase in extensor drive and a decrease in flexor drive change muscle force through contracting fibers and elastic tendons. In the pneumatic arm, the measured relation between pressure, length, and force determines how far the elbow yields under the growing load. On the CAD robot, ideal tensile actuators quantify the force each route must transmit for foot clearance and load transfer. A pneumatic design must supply that force at the lengths and pressures reached during movement.
 
 \section{Conclusion}
 
-I developed the SNS-Simscape block library and its Simulink circuit interface so that a researcher can draw a nonspiking circuit, inspect an individual connection, and generate a Simulink model from the saved circuit. The actuator and sensory blocks link muscle recruitment to force and joint motion in the single-joint demonstrations. For experiments with MuJoCo mechanics, the adapted interface sends actuator commands to a prepared model and returns joint and actuator measurements. The mechanical preparation tools make the Gait2392 human model, its rerouted robot counterpart, and a model built from the AARL robot's CAD assembly available for controller experiments.
-
-The circuit-design scripts calculate synaptic conductances for the response a researcher wants a sensory pathway to produce. The researcher can then change that pathway in a saved controller and repeat the trial with the body, neural drive, and mechanical restraints held fixed. The recordings show how that change affects neural activity, muscle force, joint motion, and foot contact. A change in muscle routing can be tested with the controller settings held fixed. The sparse neural implementation shortens repeated trials on the tested Gait2392 controller (Table~\ref{tab:sns_runtime}). These capabilities allow researchers to use human mechanics as a reference and simulate how a change in the controller or the robot's design affects coordination.
+I built tools to couple neural controllers to biomechanical models and repeat the same experiment as either the controller or the body changes. The models represent the human musculoskeletal system, its robot counterpart with rerouted muscle attachments, and the AARL robot's own mechanical design. Researchers can choose a sensory connection from the evidence in the SADb (Chapter~\ref{ch:database}), draw its circuit, and calculate or fit its synaptic strengths. Automated tuning and faster neural updates let them test alternative controllers under the same mechanical conditions.
+
+These tools bring actuator selection and neural control into the same experiment. The actuator measurements in Chapter~\ref{ch:muscles} give the force available at a specified pressure and length. The attachment geometry in Chapter~\ref{ch:torque} determines how that force acts at a joint. Together with the sensory pathways curated in Chapter~\ref{ch:database}, these relationships supply the design choices considered in Chapter~\ref{ch:overall}. Researchers can now test whether changing a sensory connection, choosing a different actuator, or moving its attachment gives the leg the torque it needs when the foot takes weight.
